  <h2>What art therapy is</h2>
  <p>
    Art therapy is a mental health profession. A trained art therapist uses art-making inside a planned, professional
    relationship, with attention to the person's history and emotional needs. The profession is regulated in some
    countries and has protected titles.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>United States:</strong> the Art Therapy Credentials Board issues the ATR (Registered Art Therapist) and
      ATR-BC (Board Certified) credentials. Check the credential with the board.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>United Kingdom:</strong> "art therapist" and "art psychotherapist" are protected titles. Practitioners must be
      registered with the Health and Care Professions Council (HCPC). Anyone can search the register.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Elsewhere:</strong> credentials and titles differ by country. Look for the national register, if there is one.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What our activities are</h2>
  <p>
    Our activities are creative, recreational and supportive. They are things to make, look at and talk about together, such
    as watercolor washes, torn paper or a memory collage. Some people find making something calming. A creative activity can
    be enjoyable, and it can also be stopped at any time.
  </p>
  <p>
    They are not art therapy. They do not assess, diagnose or treat any condition, and they make no promise about mood,
    memory, stress or any other outcome. The activities are for enjoyment and time together.
  </p>

  <h2>How to find a qualified art therapist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Ask a doctor or a licensed mental health professional for a referral if you want therapy.</li>
    <li>Check the credential or register entry yourself, before the first session.</li>
    <li>Ask about training, supervision, fees and what happens if the sessions do not help.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Our guide <a href="/activities/finding-an-art-therapist/">how to find and check an art therapist</a> lists the questions to
    ask and where to check credentials. For the difference between a profession and a creative activity, read
    <a href="/activities/art-therapy-or-creative-activity/">art therapy or a creative activity</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Creative activities you can try now</h2>
  <p>
